              "output": "<path>/home/janikvonrotz/jupyter-build/AGENTS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # AGENTS.md\n2: \n3: This file has been created from `$HOME/taskfile.build/AGENTS.md.template`.\n4: \n5: Note: @Clanker refers to the \"ai agent\" (you) who is working on this task.\n6: \n7: ## General agent guidance\n8: \n9: This section outlines the universal rules and expectations for any LLM agent working within this repository.\n10: \n11: **Safety and Quality First:** The highest priority is to produce secure, well-documented, and high-quality code. Do not introduce vulnerabilities, hardcoded secrets, or unreadable code.\n12: \n13: **Propose a Plan:** For any new task, first provide a brief plan of action. This plan should clearly outline the intended changes and the rationale behind them.\n14: \n15: **Maintain Context:** Before making any changes, an agent must read and understand the relevant files, including the project's main documentation and existing code structure.\n16: \n17: **Be Sparse:** Only introduce new code if needed. Comment only what is worth commenting. When testing focus on the business use case. \n18: \n19: ## Environment & tooling\n20: \n21: ### Taskfile\n22: \n23: This project uses the <https://taskfile.build/> standard.\n24: \n25: The `./task` file is a bash script that provides all functions necessary to work with this project.\n26: \n27: Always try to run a command with the `./task` file script.\n28: \n29: ### New Tools\n30: \n31: Do not automatically install new software packages. Always ask for permission.\n32: \n33: ### Temporary files\n34: \n35: When creating temporary fiels and scripts, use the `./tmp` directory.\n36: \n37: ### Jupyter Notebook and Regex\n38: \n39: Using regex patterns in Jupyter notebook cells is hard to edit for @Clanker tools. Create a Python file in a `./lib` folder that imports the necessary function to perform regex searches and replacements.\n40: \n41: ## Project structure\n42: \n43: @Clanker this is specific to every project. Update according to your discoveries.\n44: \n45: ## Coding conventions\n46: \n47: ### Odoo - XML / Python / Javascript\n48: \n49: When developing Odoom modules refer to the [Odoo Coding Guidelines](https://www.odoo.com/documentation/master/contributing/development/coding_guidelines.html) and the [OCA Guidelines](https://github.com/OCA/odoo-community.org/blob/master/website/Contribution/CONTRIBUTING.rst). Both documents are very detailed; here are the key points:\n50: \n51: ### XML\n52: \n53: **Module structure:** Organize files into the conventional folders (`models/`, `views/`, `wizard/`, `report/`, `security/`, `data/`, `static/`). Wizards (transient models) get their own `<name>.py` + `<name>_views.xml` pair inside `wizard/`.\n54: \n55: **Record declaration:** Use the `<record>` notation rather than the deprecated shortcuts. For field declaration, the `name` attribute always comes first, then the value (in the tag or `eval`), then other attributes ordered by importance.\n56: \n57: **Grouping:** Group records by model in the file; this convention can be relaxed when there are dependencies between actions/menus/views.\n58: \n59: **noupdate data:** Use `<data>` only to scope `noupdate=\"1\"` records. If the whole file is non-updatable, put `noupdate=\"1\"` directly on `<odoo>` and skip the `<data>` tag.\n60: \n61: **XML ID naming:** Views use `<model_name>_view_<view_type>` (kanban, form, list, search\u2026). The main action for a model is `<model_name>_action`; secondary actions get a `_<detail>` suffix. Groups use `<module_name>_group_<group_name>`, and rules use `<model_name>_rule_<concerned_group>`.\n62: \n63: **Inherited views:** Suffix the `name` field with `.inherit.<details>` so the override's purpose is clear at a glance; new primary views don't need this suffix.\n64: \n65: ### Python\n66: \n67: **Class naming:** Odoo Python classes use Pascal case (this changed from camelcase in older versions \u2014 be aware which Odoo version a codebase targets).\n68: \n69: **Model naming:** Use the singular dot notation for model names (`res.partner`, not `res.partners`), and a `<related_base_model>.report.<action>` pattern for report/SQL-view models.\n70: \n71: **Attribute/method ordering inside a model:** private attributes (`_name`, `_description`, \u2026) first, then default methods (`_default_*`), then field declarations, then compute/onchange/constraint methods, then CRUD overrides, then action methods, and finally other business methods. Action methods are prefixed `action_`, operate on a single record, and should call `self.ensure_one()` first.\n72: \n73: **Variable naming:** Suffix variables with `_id`/`_ids` only when they hold an actual id or list of ids \u2014 not a recordset. One2many/Many2many fields always take the `_ids` suffix; Many2one fields take `_id`.\n74: \n75: **Style baseline:** Follow PEP8 (4-space indents, grouped/ordered imports: stdlib, third-party, local).\n76: \n77: **Odoo idioms:** Avoid creating your own generators and decorators \u2014 use only the ones the Odoo API provides \u2014 and prefer `filtered`, `mapped`, `sorted`, etc. over manual loops for readability and performance. Remember the context is a frozendict and cannot be mutated.\n78: \n79: **Documentation:** Document code with docstrings on methods and comments on tricky parts.\n80: \n81: ### JavaScript\n82: \n83: **Static layout:** Files in a module's `static/` folder are served at a URL prefixed with the module name (e.g. `static/src/js/some_file.js` \u2192 `your-server.com/<module>/static/src/js/some_file.js`). Third-party JS libraries go under `static/lib/<libname>/`.\n84: \n85: **File organization:** Each component lives in its own, meaningfully-named file, mirroring the same logic used for Python models; subdirectories structure larger \"packages.\" The same per-component-file logic applies to QWeb templates and SCSS.\n86: \n87: **Class naming:** Prefix CSS/JS classes with `o_<module_name>` (technical module name, or main route for website-style modules).\n88: \n89: **Assets:** Never link to external images or libraries by URL \u2014 vendor them into the codebase instead.\n90: \n91: A few of these (Pascal case for Python classes, `tree` vs `list` view type) changed between Odoo versions, so worth double-checking the exact version your guideline doc targets.\n92: \n93: ### Bash\n94: \n95: The following guideline is heavily based on the [Bash Style Guide | ysap.sh](https://style.ysap.sh/), which should be considered the primary reference for style. Adhere to the following principles to ensure scripts are safe, predictable, and maintainable.\n96: \n97: **Shebang:** Start scripts with `#!/usr/bin/env bash` for portability.\n98: \n99: **Quoting:** This is critical.\n100: \n101: - Use double quotes `\"` for strings that require variable expansion.\n102: - Use single quotes `'` for all other strings.\n103: \n104: **Always quote variable expansions** `\"$var\"` to prevent word-splitting and globbing issues.\n105: \n106: **Variables:** Distinct between local and environment vars.\n107: \n108: - Use `local` for all variables inside functions.\n109: - Use lowercase names for local variables (e.g., `buildx_output`).\n110: - Use uppercase names for environment variables (e.g. `export BUILDX_PLATFORM`)\n111: \n112: **Functions:**\n113: \n114: - Do not use the `function` keyword.\n115: - Use `my-func() { ... }` syntax.\n116: - Function names are in kebab case.\n117: \n118: **Conditionals:**\n119: \n120: - Always use `[[ ... ]]` for conditional testing, not `[ ... ]` or `test`.\n121: - Use `((...))` for arithmetic comparisons (e.g., `((a > b))`).\n122: - When replacing `[]` with `[[]]` make sure to escape the brackets. The `[]` is a regex filter.\n123: \n124: **Command Substitution:**\n125: \n126: - Always use `$(...)` for command substitution, not backticks.\n127: \n128: **Arrays:**\n129: \n130: - Use Bash arrays to manage lists of items instead of space-separated strings.\n131: - Iterate over arrays using `for item in \"${my_array[@]}\"; do ... done`.\n132: \n133: **Avoid External Commands:**\n134: \n135: - Use Bash's built-in parameter expansion for string manipulation (e.g., `${var/foo/bar}`).\n136: - Use globbing (`*`) to iterate over files, not `ls`.\n137: - Avoid `cat` when a command can read a file directly (e.g., `grep \"pattern\" file`).\n138: \n139: **eval:** Never use `eval`.\n140: \n141: **Logging:**\n142: \n143: - Use single quotes for outputs without variables.\n144: - Use double quotes for outputs with variables.\n145: - Do not finish sentence with a dot if action is following (e.g. `\"Backup file\"`).\n146: - Finish sentence with a dot if echo is after action (e.g. `\"Backup finished.\"`).\n\n(End of file - total 146 lines)\n</content>",
